 was in  fact the spirit of the ship  struggling to stay together for them  because it really truly cared about them  which not only humanized the ship but  also allows us to be thankful towards  Frankie for sharing that information so  kind-hearted Lee to us up who's clearly  struggling with it through this we  learned that Frankie is actually a  really chill and honest responsible guy  obviously the word honest is him  quotations there and what's more is that  we get a fantastically compelling  backstory for him where and we learned  that he's the one that has the  blueprints he grew up under the man who  made the seafaring train and that guy  was the one who made gol D Roger ship  and what's more is that the antagonist  from the government in that flashback  that killed his father figure Tom is now  behind the cp9 situation a tennis lobby  see what I mean about all of these  seemingly unrelated plot threads  developing in real time and clashing  with each other in natural ways well it  just keeps happening  the cp9 bossed into their workshop and  kidnap both Frankie a noose up but not  before sending the merry go crashing to  the sea below which not only makes me  want these guys to be eviscerated from  existence but now they're on the trade  with Robin and what's more is that they  are heading to a place called in his  lobby where in addition to no one ever  really returning from their alive is  also the place where Frankie's father  Tom was sent  that's where Robins being sent to also I  just love this story that all converged  right at the end but not before leaving  our heroes in dire straits and it's  moments like this that make me think God  it's so difficult to choose a favorite  among these characters they're all so  perfectly realized and enjoyable in  their own different ways but I think  when I turn the page and realize that  son  gee all this time had been waiting  outside the train for Robin to arrive  and leaving I might have fallen in love  with him there and then it's still  between him and Nusa but I just adore  Sanjay he's the perfect blend of flawed  cool and he absolutely drives the plot  for in ways few other characters do in  this series the Train the Train sequence  in this arc might be the best set-piece  idea in all of one piece I've  experienced so far it's straightforward  to understand in that Sanjay needs to  make his way through the train cars to  reach Robin who stopped in Frankie  there's a clear goal for Luffy and the  rest of the crew to pursue them in order  to give Sanji backup there's a built-in  time limit as they are about to arrive  at in its Lobby shortly the gang is  separated the villains are together and  the motivations are clear save Robin  save us up and save Frankie before the  blueprints fall into the wrong hands and  boy was I rooting for Sanji during this  one the setup is honestly great - with  each subsequent car in the Train filled  with more and more dangerous folks but  once angie reaches OSAP and Frankie who  were held towards the back of the Train  where he initially hit the strategies  implemented our clever and satisfying to  watch with the majority of the bad guys  lured to the back of the Train before  being cut loose entirely and what I love  this isn't just because as a clever plan  on behalf of Sanji it's that it also  highlights why this particular action  sequence is so interesting and fun in  the first place as luffy's or onami  choppered the water seven shipwrights  and the Frankie family are following in  another train behind them and because  there's only one train track it means  that anything that is caught loose will  eventually have to be dealt with by  Luffy and the gang as they chase after  their friends honestly it's an  incredibly creative sequence and the  best part is still to come  you see once Sanji saved Frankie and  Aesop despite wanting to help out Robin  after understanding the situation Musab  still felt tremendous shame and so  instead 